ZTEXT001000019cc3711f9480000000
Zero pixel desktop, visible console and keyboard

kernel.zero implements the display and keyboard protocol logic in Zero.
No assembly driver, external font file, terminal library or runtime is used.
The original framebuffer glyph table contains seven five-bit rows per ASCII
character 32..126, stored as byte values 48+row. Its renderer scales rows twice
vertically and adds margins to form8x16 logical cells. At1920x1080 the
window compositor doubles both axes to produce16x32 physical cells.

BIOS first attempts VBE true-color graphics and otherwise uses VGA text
memory at 0xb8000 in 80x25 mode. UEFI supplies a validated GOP framebuffer.
Both pixel paths use 32-bit RGB/BGR words and honor stride; the reserved
fourth byte is not an alpha-compositing interface. The preferred mode is
1920x1080. BIOS next tries800x600, then a supported640..800 by480..600
fallback. GOP ranks800x600,640x480 and other validated smaller modes after
Full HD; it revalidates the actual current mode after selection.
See BIOS-GRAPHICS.ztext/UEFI-CONSOLE.ztext.

The window compositor draws terminal frames, a focus border, titles and
keyboard hints. Tile mode lays out groups of up to three terminals; scroll
mode centers the focused column and clips its neighbors. See WINDOWS.ztext.
Each window has a7168-byte output ring and its own input/selected volume.
The compositor reflows each visible ring into an at-most96x48 character grid
and draws bounded8x16 logical cells. Exact1920x1080 uses a960x540 logical
workspace with2x2 physical pixels per logical pixel; other modes retain1x.
Font/background fills respect the physical stride and RGB/BGR order; the
glyph itself is5x7 with doubled logical scanlines. A caret marks focus.
Dirty windows repaint as needed; layout generation checks prevent a yielded
renderer from continuing to paint a closed or reused slot. The old single
terminal is retained as a fallback if no safe session pool can be allocated.
VGA fallback presents only the focused session, with a title and80x24 body.
No firmware service is called by the kernel after entry.

CR, LF, backspace, printable ASCII, wrapping, scrolling and clear are handled
directly. LF starts a new line at column0; existing CRLF does the same. COM1
adds CR before a lone LF and preserves existing CRLF, including across message
boundaries. VGA text mode also updates the hardware cursor. The framebuffer
renderer can blink its focused caret using the polled clock. Mouse input
and a general application graphics API are not implemented.

animate and theme toggle caret blinking and desktop colors. pixel
X Y R G B writes one pixel with bounded decimal coordinates and 0..255 RGB
values. These coordinates are physical pixels even with2x interface scaling.
pause 2 freezes the graphics worker; resume 2 lets it continue.
Input/commands still run while that worker is paused; buffered output becomes
visible after resume. Manual pixels are
temporary and can be covered by future drawing. In VGA or serial fallback,
pixel commands report that no supported linear framebuffer is available.

The kernel initializes the 8042 controller and the first PS/2 port, selects
keyboard scan set 2 and controller translation to set 1, and polls events.
The US ASCII layout supports both Shift keys, Caps Lock, ordinary Enter,
keypad Enter, Backspace and Tab as one space. F1 creates a window, F2 closes,
F3/F4 changes focus and F5 toggles layouts. Function-key repeats are latched
until key release. Pause and unsupported extended
keys are ignored. The auxiliary PS/2 port is disabled; mouse input is not
implemented. Controller waits are bounded, so absence/failure does not block
the kernel indefinitely. No keyboard interrupt handler is required.

COM1 at 115200 baud, 8N1 remains an optional mirrored output and input source.
The UART scratch register is probed first. Absent or stalled UART output is
disabled without stopping the visible terminal. The serial and PS/2 inputs
feed the same command parser and preserve its line-length and CRLF behavior.
Serial CRLF framing and keyboard modifiers remain global across focus; input
line contents and the overflow flag remain private to the selected session.

Loader handoff, all u64 fields at these byte offsets:
+48 display kind: 0 serial only, 1 VGA text, 2 linear framebuffer
+56 framebuffer address (0xb8000 for VGA text)
+64 framebuffer width in pixels
+72 framebuffer height in pixels
+80 framebuffer stride in pixels
+88 framebuffer pixel format: 0 RGB, 1 BGR
+96 boot platform: 1 BIOS, 2 UEFI (reserved for diagnostics)
+104 execution architecture: 32 or 64
+112 loader-owned kernel stack-pool base
+120 stack-pool size in bytes
+128/+136 native compiler bundle address/size
+144/+152 native compiler workspace address/size

The loader must ensure the framebuffer address is mapped, the reported size
covers stride*height*4, and the supplied mode remains active after firmware
services end. Kernel checks restrict accepted framebuffer dimensions to
640..8192 by 400..8192, stride between width and 16384, and format 0 or 1.
The kernel arena is64KiB; display/input state follows the unchanged ZeroFS,
line and listing buffers. Thread contexts and desktop counters also
live in shared console state. The first24KiB layout and ZeroFS format remain
unchanged; the remaining40KiB holds the filesystem request/workspace and
two16KiB metadata buffers. The prompt identifies the selected root, A:|>.
GO changes roots; paths use a vertical bar, not a slash. See DRIVES.ztext.

See verification.ztext for the final integrated BIOS/UEFI/VirtualBox test scope.

The wifi command temporarily uses the focused window for wireless setup.
Up/Down selects, Enter advances, Backspace edits the masked password and Esc
closes the app. Closing restores the window history and erases the password.
Function keys do not create or switch windows while this modal app is open.
Detection currently reports unsupported or absent PCI wireless adapters;
there is no native radio connection driver yet. net/wifi-fixtures images
are labeled simulations, separate from real no-adapter VM captures.

The pixel command uses one-based physical coordinates: X1..width and
Y1..height. RGB values remain0..255. Internal framebuffer offsets remain
zero-based; this user-facing change begins in0.9.0.

apps, system, storage and network use the same bounded modal frame. Selection
starts at1. Enter opens/toggles/selects the displayed action; Backspace returns
to the application menu, Esc restores terminal history. Network addresses are
read from the actual DHCP state. Storage lists actual discovered volume roots.
